1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,690
Sam didn't expect that.

2
00:00:01,690 --> 00:00:04,487
Meta's clinical cut had just won there, clearly.

3
00:00:04,487 --> 00:00:06,468
TikTok's raw cut won here instead.

4
00:00:06,468 --> 00:00:09,556
Section 5.1 asked you to check ratios and safe zones.

5
00:00:09,556 --> 00:00:14,392
TikTok asks something harder: whether your ad reads as a TikTok in the first place,

6
00:00:14,392 --> 00:00:17,189
before anyone gets far enough to judge the hook.

7
00:00:17,189 --> 00:00:21,617
As of this recording, native, creator-style content consistently outperforms

8
00:00:21,617 --> 00:00:26,511
studio-polished footage here, for a specific reason: TikTok's audience has a trained

9
00:00:26,511 --> 00:00:31,114
reflex for skipping anything that announces itself as an ad in the first frame.

10
00:00:31,114 --> 00:00:34,610
A cut that looks like an ordinary post survives that reflex.

11
00:00:34,610 --> 00:00:37,115
A cut that looks like a commercial doesn't.

12
00:00:37,115 --> 00:00:40,087
What actually earns that pass past the skip reflex?

13
00:00:40,087 --> 00:00:40,961
Not sloppiness.

14
00:00:40,961 --> 00:00:45,680
The best-performing native content is casual in look, not in construction: a real

15
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:50,342
hook line in the first second, legible on-screen text sitting inside the frame's

16
00:00:50,342 --> 00:00:53,255
safe zone, clear audio, a specific call to action.

17
00:00:53,255 --> 00:00:56,110
The difference from a studio ad isn't less craft.

18
00:00:56,110 --> 00:01:00,829
It's craft that hides itself behind the look of something a viewer wasn't paid to

19
00:01:00,829 --> 00:01:01,121
make.

20
00:01:01,121 --> 00:01:04,151
That's good news for the workflow you already built.

21
00:01:04,151 --> 00:01:08,987
An avatar-led, sound-off-designed ad from Section 4.5 was never trying to look like

22
00:01:08,987 --> 00:01:09,744
a commercial.

23
00:01:09,744 --> 00:01:12,424
TikTok doesn't ask you to build something new.

24
00:01:12,424 --> 00:01:15,745
It asks you to stop softening the one you've already got.

25
00:01:16,295 --> 00:01:20,911
Here's the format built specifically around the grammar this lecture just named.

26
00:01:20,911 --> 00:01:23,622
A Spark Ad isn't a new upload dressed as an ad.

27
00:01:23,622 --> 00:01:28,468
It's an existing post, boosted, keeping every social signal that made it look like a

28
00:01:28,468 --> 00:01:33,141
real post in the first place: the account name, the sound, the likes and comments

29
00:01:33,141 --> 00:01:34,410
already sitting on it.

30
00:01:34,410 --> 00:01:37,007
That matters mechanically, not just visually.

31
00:01:37,007 --> 00:01:41,853
As of this recording, TikTok's delivery reads engagement on the underlying post as a

32
00:01:41,853 --> 00:01:42,256
signal.

33
00:01:42,256 --> 00:01:47,102
An ad built from a post that already earned real engagement organically gets matched

34
00:01:47,102 --> 00:01:51,660
to similar viewers faster than an ad starting cold with zero history behind it.

35
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:56,448
Notice what this changes about how you'd actually use your Section 4 workflow here.

36
00:01:56,448 --> 00:02:01,179
On Meta, you generate a batch, upload it, and let the delivery system sort winners

37
00:02:01,179 --> 00:02:01,871
from losers.

38
00:02:01,871 --> 00:02:06,544
On TikTok, the strongest move is often to post an angle organically first, let it

39
00:02:06,544 --> 00:02:11,275
earn real engagement on its own, and only then spend behind whichever one actually

40
00:02:11,275 --> 00:02:11,794
took off.

41
00:02:11,794 --> 00:02:13,525
The generation stays the same.

42
00:02:13,525 --> 00:02:16,467
The path from generated clip to paid spend doesn't.

43
00:02:16,467 --> 00:02:18,198
That's not a small difference.

44
00:02:18,198 --> 00:02:22,813
Your posting calendar becomes part of your testing architecture here in a way it

45
00:02:22,813 --> 00:02:23,852
never was on Meta.

46
00:02:23,852 --> 00:02:28,467
An angle ignored organically is telling you something before you've spent a cent

47
00:02:28,467 --> 00:02:31,179
finding that out through paid delivery instead.

48
00:02:31,729 --> 00:02:36,366
This is the part that catches people who've only ever run Meta ads, where you simply

49
00:02:36,366 --> 00:02:37,138
upload a file.

50
00:02:37,138 --> 00:02:39,567
Spark Ads need permission, not just footage.

51
00:02:39,567 --> 00:02:44,094
A creator generates their own authorization code from inside the app, tied to that

52
00:02:44,094 --> 00:02:46,081
specific post, for a limited window.

53
00:02:46,081 --> 00:02:50,497
You enter that code in Ads Manager, and only then can the post run as paid media

54
00:02:50,497 --> 00:02:51,491
under your budget.

55
00:02:51,491 --> 00:02:55,907
Miss the expiry and the code stops working, mid-flight, whether or not the ad is

56
00:02:55,907 --> 00:02:56,846
still performing.

57
00:02:56,846 --> 00:02:59,440
Two practical habits follow directly from that.

58
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:04,022
First, track expiry dates the same way you'd track a licence tier from Section 4.1,

59
00:03:04,022 --> 00:03:06,506
in the same file as the brief, not in memory.

60
00:03:06,506 --> 00:03:11,033
Second, when you're using your own brand account rather than an outside creator's,

61
00:03:11,033 --> 00:03:15,449
you're both the poster and the authoriser, which simplifies the flow but doesn't

62
00:03:15,449 --> 00:03:16,332
remove the step.

63
00:03:16,332 --> 00:03:20,803
The code still has to be generated and entered, even when you're authorising your

64
00:03:20,803 --> 00:03:21,963
own post to yourself.

65
00:03:22,513 --> 00:03:26,717
This is exactly where Harlan's own set stopped behaving the same way on both

66
00:03:26,717 --> 00:03:27,271
platforms.

67
00:03:27,271 --> 00:03:31,807
The clinical framing that performed on Meta, credentials and a spec upfront, reads

68
00:03:31,807 --> 00:03:33,467
as slightly too polished here.

69
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:37,837
The raw, unframed cut wins instead, the one that plays like a real shift ending

70
00:03:37,837 --> 00:03:40,327
rather than a brand making a case for itself.

71
00:03:40,327 --> 00:03:43,647
Why would the same claim, the same product, split like that?

72
00:03:43,647 --> 00:03:48,294
Because Meta's feed still tolerates a moment that reads as an advertisement, if it's

73
00:03:48,294 --> 00:03:48,902
a good one.

74
00:03:48,902 --> 00:03:51,337
TikTok's doesn't tolerate it nearly as well.

75
00:03:51,337 --> 00:03:54,490
The platform didn't change what's true about the product.

76
00:03:54,490 --> 00:03:58,252
It changed which true thing lands as a real post instead of a pitch.

77
00:03:58,252 --> 00:04:02,014
Notice what didn't change: the claims boundary underneath both cuts.

78
00:04:02,014 --> 00:04:06,495
The raw version doesn't say anything the credentialed version couldn't defend, it

79
00:04:06,495 --> 00:04:11,143
just says less of it, and lets the moment carry what the credentials would otherwise

80
00:04:11,143 --> 00:04:12,415
have to state outright.

81
00:04:12,415 --> 00:04:15,126
That's worth holding onto going into Section six.

82
00:04:15,126 --> 00:04:19,441
A cut that reads as more casual isn't automatically a looser cut, claims-wise.

83
00:04:19,441 --> 00:04:23,535
It can be the exact same claim, delivered with less scaffolding around it.

84
00:04:24,085 --> 00:04:28,911
One honest note that applies to almost everything you've built since Section three.

85
00:04:28,911 --> 00:04:33,504
TikTok's policy on AI-generated and AI-edited content requires a clear label or

86
00:04:33,504 --> 00:04:36,412
disclosure on ad creative, not just organic posts.

87
00:04:36,412 --> 00:04:41,295
That covers generated avatars, generated statics, and anything significantly altered

88
00:04:41,295 --> 00:04:44,842
by AI, which is most of what this course teaches you to make.

89
00:04:44,842 --> 00:04:46,935
Section 6.3 builds this in properly.

90
00:04:46,935 --> 00:04:51,645
For now, just hold the fact: disclosure on TikTok isn't a nice-to-have layered on

91
00:04:51,645 --> 00:04:52,866
top of a finished ad.

92
00:04:52,866 --> 00:04:55,947
It's part of what makes the ad allowed to run at all.

93
00:04:55,947 --> 00:05:00,831
Think about why this rule exists on this specific platform, more than somewhere your

94
00:05:00,831 --> 00:05:03,331
ad announces itself as a commercial anyway.

95
00:05:03,331 --> 00:05:06,994
TikTok's whole grammar is built on ads that pass as real posts.

96
00:05:06,994 --> 00:05:11,529
A platform that rewards that illusion has an obvious reason to require the one

97
00:05:11,529 --> 00:05:15,425
honest signal that tells a viewer what they're actually looking at.

98
00:05:15,425 --> 00:05:19,960
The disclosure requirement and the native-grammar advantage aren't in tension.

99
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:24,553
The second one is exactly why the first one matters here more than most places.

100
00:05:25,103 --> 00:05:29,647
Next lecture takes on a platform with a genuinely different logic again: someone

101
00:05:29,647 --> 00:05:34,360
arriving on YouTube usually already wants something, which changes what a hook even

102
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:36,121
needs to do in the first place.

103
00:05:36,121 --> 00:05:40,721
Before that, if you're running Harlan's raw cut, or your own equivalent of it, on

104
00:05:40,721 --> 00:05:45,321
TikTok, go check your Spark Ads code's expiry today, and log the date in the same

105
00:05:45,321 --> 00:05:46,627
file as your B04 brief.

106
00:05:46,627 --> 00:05:51,114
It's the kind of detail that's invisible until the exact moment a live ad stops

107
00:05:51,114 --> 00:05:55,657
serving mid-flight, and the fix takes thirty seconds now against an afternoon of

108
00:05:55,657 --> 00:05:57,417
confused troubleshooting later.
